  "text": "Let's start by determining the required ratio for each kind of food item across all groups. \n\nAthlete boxes contain 3 chicken, 2 vegetarian, and 3 salads. Coach boxes contain 2 chicken, 1 vegetarian, and 2 salads. Individual packages for officials can be 1 chicken, 1 vegetarian, or 1 salad. \n\nTo ensure they can supply the same variety of food items for each group, we need to find LCMs of these quantities to get whole numbers that work for all groups. This will ensure an equal distribution of each kind of food item across the groups. \n\nFirst, let's determine the LCMs:\n\n1. Chicken: Athletes: 3, Coaches: 2, Officials: 1\n   - LCM of 3, 2, 1 is 48 (to ensure all meals give the same quantity of chicken)\n\n2. Vegetarian sandwiches: Athletes: 2, Coaches: 1, Officials: 1\n   - LCM of 2, 1, 1 is 2 (ensure the LCM is the smallest number that works for all)\n\n3. Salad: Athletes: 3, Coaches: 2\n   - LCM of 3, 2 is 6 (ensure the LCM is the smallest number that works for all)\n\nNow, we calculate the total meals for each kind:\n\n- Chicken: LCM gives us 3 athletes, 2 coaches, and 3 officials\n  - Athletes: 3 * 8 people = 24 meals\n  - Coaches: 2 * 4 people = 8 meals\n  - Officials: 3 * 1 package/people = 3 meals (since we need whole number systems)\n\n- Vegetarian: 2 * 8 people = 16 meals \n  and 1 * 4 people = 4 meals\n  - Total vegetarian = 16 + 4 = 20 meals \n\n- Salad: 3 * 8 people = 24 meals\n  - Since bigger box 3 times smaller box = 8 * 96 times ladies card\n  - Since smaller box 3 times smaller card = 12.\n\nThe total meals by great number shouldeq. chicken: 48*3=144 meals; vegetarian: 20 meals; salad 24 * 3=72 mealy.\n\n* The official boxes: 96 := 16 + 48 => 3 *\"nature card\" might needs 3 *72* 3 = 648! \n\n*) −\n\n*‏\n\n.end",
